      generic: "nephron diuretic site map",
      aliases: ["diuretic nephron map", "where diuretics work", "sites of diuretic action", "proximal loop distal collecting duct diuretics", "diuretic mechanism map"],
      class: "Mechanism map linking each diuretic class to its nephron segment",
      classExampleNames: ["Acetazolamide", "Furosemide", "Hydrochlorothiazide", "Spironolactone", "Amiloride", "Mannitol", "Tolvaptan"],
      classExampleKeys: ["acetazolamide", "furosemide", "hydrochlorothiazide", "spironolactone", "amiloride", "mannitol", "tolvaptan"],
      classPathway: ["Proximal tubule to collecting duct", "Transporter-specific sodium or water handling", "Predictable electrolyte and acid-base signatures"],
      usedToTreat: "A study and clinical-reasoning map for edema, hypertension, heart failure congestion, selected electrolyte disorders, raised intracranial or intraocular pressure, glaucoma, altitude illness, and euvolemic or hypervolemic hyponatremia. It explains why drugs that all increase urine output are not interchangeable.",
      description: "A diuretic is defined by where and how it changes tubular solute or water transport. Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule and wastes bicarbonate. Loop drugs block NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, where a large sodium load and the medullary gradient are generated, so they produce powerful natriuresis. Thiazides block NCC in the distal convoluted tubule and are especially useful for blood pressure. Spironolactone and eplerenone block the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor, while amiloride and triamterene directly block ENaC in the collecting duct; both conserve potassium but act at different molecular steps. Mannitol is filtered and not reabsorbed, so it holds water osmotically within the tubule. Tolvaptan blocks vasopressin V2 signaling and removes water with relatively little sodium. Locating the target predicts both the benefit and the adverse pattern before a list is memorized.",
      mechanism: "The proximal tubule reabsorbs most filtered sodium and bicarbonate, but downstream segments can reclaim much of a proximal sodium leak; this is why acetazolamide is a modest natriuretic and self-limits as bicarbonate falls. The thick ascending limb reabsorbs sodium, potassium, and chloride through NKCC2 and is water impermeable. Blocking it both wastes salt and collapses the corticomedullary gradient, making loop diuretics potent. The distal NCC segment handles less sodium, so thiazides are weaker diuretics but have durable antihypertensive effects. In the collecting duct, ENaC sodium entry makes the lumen negative and electrically favors potassium and hydrogen secretion. Blocking aldosterone or ENaC therefore saves potassium and hydrogen, creating hyperkalemia and acidosis risk. V2 signaling normally inserts aquaporin-2 water channels; vaptans interrupt that water-only pathway. This segment-to-consequence chain explains why combining classes can overcome compensation but can also rapidly deplete sodium, potassium, magnesium, chloride, water, and kidney perfusion.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("This physiology card is not a product label. The high-alert principle is that urine output alone does not prove safe or effective decongestion. A patient can produce urine while becoming hypotensive, alkalotic, dysnatremic, or azotemic, so weight, examination, intake/output, pressure, kidney function, and electrolytes must be interpreted together."),
      adverseEffects: ["Loop and thiazide pathways commonly produce volume depletion, hyponatremia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis because distal sodium delivery and RAAS activation increase potassium and hydrogen loss.", "MRA and ENaC blockade reverse distal potassium secretion and can cause life-threatening hyperkalemia; carbonic-anhydrase inhibition causes bicarbonate loss and non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis; vaptans can overcorrect sodium; mannitol can first expand plasma volume and later dehydrate it."],
      contraindications: ["The map does not replace the individual label. Anuria, severe electrolyte depletion, unstable pressure, evolving acute kidney injury, hepatic encephalopathy risk, pregnancy, and major drug interactions have class-specific implications.", "Do not stack agents simply because they act at different sites. Sequential blockade is reserved for a defined indication with a monitoring plan because downstream compensation is also a safety buffer."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Name the segment, transporter or receptor, expected urine effect, and expected serum pattern before administration. That four-step prediction makes a new laboratory change recognizable as pharmacology rather than surprise.", "Trend daily weight under comparable conditions, congestion findings, orthostasis, intake/output, urine response after dosing, creatinine/eGFR, sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate, and magnesium. Match the monitoring interval to acuity and route."],
      interactions: ["NSAIDs reduce renal prostaglandin signaling and can blunt natriuresis while worsening kidney perfusion; RAAS blockers add pressure, potassium, and filtration effects; lithium clearance can fall when sodium handling changes.", "Aminoglycosides or other ototoxins increase loop-related hearing risk. Potassium supplements, salt substitutes, trimethoprim, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNIs, MRAs, and ENaC blockers can converge on dangerous hyperkalemia."],
      keyLabs: ["Weight, edema or jugular venous pressure, oxygenation, blood pressure and orthostasis, urine output, sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate, magnesium, calcium when relevant, BUN, creatinine/eGFR, and osmolality for hyperosmolar or aquaretic therapy.", "Interpret creatinine in context. A small change during successful decongestion may be hemodynamic, while progressive azotemia with hypotension, oliguria, poor perfusion, or disappearing urine response suggests harm or treatment failure."],
