      name: "Loop diuretic equivalence, bioavailability, and tubular delivery",
      generic: "loop diuretic equivalence bioavailability tubular delivery",
      aliases: ["loop diuretic conversion", "Lasix Bumex Demadex conversion", "40 furosemide 1 bumetanide 20 torsemide", "why loop diuretics stop working", "loop diuretic absorption comparison"],
      class: "Dose-conversion and pharmacokinetic reasoning pathway",
      classExampleNames: ["Furosemide", "Bumetanide", "Torsemide", "Ethacrynic acid"],
      classExampleKeys: ["furosemide", "bumetanide", "torsemide", "ethacrynic acid"],
      classPathway: ["Approximate equipotent dosing", "Oral absorption and proximal tubular secretion", "Threshold and ceiling response"],
      usedToTreat: "A conversion and troubleshooting card for changing loop drugs or routes and for evaluating an apparently poor diuretic response. It is educational; actual conversion, IV strategy, and monitoring follow the local protocol and patient response.",
      description: "A commonly used clinical approximation is furosemide 40 mg orally, furosemide 20 mg intravenously, bumetanide 1 mg orally or intravenously, and torsemide 20 mg orally or intravenously. These are starting equivalences, not physical constants. Furosemide oral bioavailability is variable and gut edema can make it less predictable; torsemide is usually absorbed more consistently and bumetanide is potent in small milligram doses. IV administration bypasses intestinal absorption but does not bypass the need for renal perfusion and proximal tubular secretion. A dose below the patient's natriuretic threshold may produce almost no response, while doses above the ceiling add toxicity more readily than natriuresis. The right question is therefore not only 'Which dose is equal?' but 'Did enough active drug reach the luminal transporter for long enough, and what compensatory sodium retention followed?'",
      mechanism: "Loop drugs circulate highly albumin bound, enter proximal tubular cells through basolateral organic-anion transport, and are secreted into the lumen where NKCC2 is accessible. CKD reduces functioning nephron mass and tubular secretion; endogenous organic acids compete for transport; NSAIDs reduce renal blood flow and prostaglandin-supported natriuresis; hypoalbuminemia changes delivery and distribution; bowel edema delays or reduces oral absorption. After a short loop effect ends, the kidney can retain sodium during the post-diuretic period, especially if dietary sodium is high. Repeated exposure also enlarges distal sodium-reabsorbing capacity. These mechanisms explain why changing route, shortening the interval, increasing an inadequate threshold dose, reducing sodium intake, or adding a carefully monitored distal agent can work. They also explain why simply increasing a dose without confirming adherence, perfusion, absorption, timing, urine sodium or output, and congestion can be dangerous.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Equivalence values are approximate and protocol dependent. Never perform a blind conversion without reviewing indication, route, kidney function, prior response, blood pressure, current congestion, electrolytes, and the monitoring environment."),
      adverseEffects: ["Conversion that overestimates need can cause abrupt volume contraction, hypotension, acute kidney injury, electrolyte loss, alkalosis, and ototoxic peak exposure.", "Conversion that underestimates the patient's threshold can leave pulmonary or systemic congestion untreated; repeated ineffective low doses may delay escalation to an effective strategy."],
      contraindications: ["Do not use an equivalence table as an order in an unstable patient. Anuria, shock, severe depletion, rapidly worsening kidney function, and uncertain medication reconciliation require direct clinical review.", "Ethacrynic acid should not be casually substituted solely because a patient reports a vague sulfa allergy; clarify the reaction and weigh its distinctive diarrhea and hearing risks."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Document the old drug, dose, route, timing, urine response, weight trend, and reason for conversion. Reassess after the expected onset and chart both efficacy and safety endpoints.", "For suspected resistance, verify administration and sodium intake, inspect perfusion and congestion, review NSAIDs and albumin, and obtain ordered spot urine sodium or timed urine output rather than labeling the patient resistant from one late observation."],
      interactions: ["NSAIDs, low renal perfusion, and competing organic anions reduce luminal delivery. Sucralfate reduces furosemide absorption and should be separated according to labeling.", "Sequential nephron blockade can restore response but multiplies sodium, potassium, chloride, magnesium, pressure, and kidney-function risk."],
      keyLabs: ["Weight, blood pressure, orthostasis, urine output over a defined interval, sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate, magnesium, BUN, creatinine/eGFR, and optional urine sodium under the treatment protocol.", "Compare equivalent exposure with actual response. A table cannot account for gut edema, CKD, albumin, route, timing, or the patient's previous dose-response curve."],
      nclexTraps: ["One milligram of bumetanide is not a tiny dose relative to 40 mg of oral furosemide; potency is molecule specific.", "Torsemide's more predictable absorption does not prove superior survival. The pragmatic TRANSFORM-HF trial did not show mortality superiority over furosemide."],
      sourceNote: "Current U.S. loop labels, AHA/ACC/HFSA guidance, and TRANSFORM-HF summary: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=916de29e-228c-4dfc-bcee-ad9a641316bd ; https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=a3d68763-95e0-4ad6-89c2-c4b1c0b43948 ; https://newsroom.heart.org/news/transform-hf-trial-found-no-difference-in-effectiveness-between-2-common-loop-diuretics",
      tags: ["frontier-wave15", "loop conversion", "furosemide equivalent", "bumetanide potency", "torsemide bioavailability", "diuretic resistance"]
