      aliases: ["Lasix", "furesomide", "ferosemide", "frusemide", "furosemide loop diuretic", "Lasix water pill"],
      brandExamples: ["Lasix"],
      class: "Sulfonamide loop diuretic with variable oral absorption",
      classPathway: ["Thick ascending limb", "Luminal NKCC2 inhibition", "Potent sodium-chloride loss with medullary-gradient disruption"],
      usedToTreat: "Edema associated with heart failure, cirrhosis, and renal disease including nephrotic syndrome in adults and pediatric patients; adult hypertension under current oral labeling. IV formulations are used when a rapid or parenteral loop effect is clinically required.",
      description: "Furosemide, commonly called Lasix, is a high-ceiling loop diuretic that removes sodium and water by blocking NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb. It is often the first familiar drug for pulmonary or systemic congestion, but it does not directly strengthen the heart or repair the kidney; it lowers venous filling pressure and tissue fluid while the underlying disease is treated. Oral absorption is variable, with current tablet labeling reporting mean bioavailability around 64 percent of IV exposure in fasting healthy subjects. Gut edema, kidney disease, low perfusion, NSAIDs, and hypoalbuminemia can make the response less predictable. Oral effect begins within about one hour, peaks in the first or second hour, and lasts roughly six to eight hours. That short action permits post-diuretic sodium retention later in the day, explaining why timing, dose interval, dietary sodium, and measured response matter.",
      mechanism: "Highly albumin-bound furosemide reaches the proximal tubule through blood flow and is actively secreted into the tubular lumen through organic-anion transport. From the luminal side it inhibits NKCC2, reducing sodium, potassium, and chloride reabsorption. Less ROMK potassium recycling removes the positive luminal voltage that normally drives calcium and magnesium reabsorption, so both can be lost. More sodium reaches the collecting duct; ENaC reabsorbs it, making the lumen negative and increasing potassium and hydrogen secretion. Volume contraction simultaneously activates RAAS and aldosterone. This combined distal-delivery and hormonal effect explains hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis. Reduced medullary salt accumulation weakens urinary concentration. A patient with CKD may need a larger threshold dose because less active drug reaches fewer functioning nephrons, but dose escalation still increases electrolyte, pressure, renal, and hearing risk.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("The U.S. label carries a prominent warning that excessive furosemide can produce profound diuresis with water and electrolyte depletion. Individualize dose and schedule, and do not interpret brisk urine as automatically beneficial. Rapid IV delivery, severe renal impairment, high doses, hypoproteinemia, or concurrent aminoglycoside, cisplatin, ethacrynic acid, or other ototoxic exposure increases tinnitus and permanent or reversible hearing-loss risk."),
