      description: "Bumetanide, historically branded Bumex, is a potent loop diuretic that blocks NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, causing rapid sodium, chloride, and water loss. Roughly 1 mg of bumetanide produces a loop effect comparable to about 40 mg oral furosemide in common clinical conversion tables, so its small milligram number must never be read as a small pharmacologic dose. Oral absorption is generally rapid and more predictable than furosemide; diuresis usually begins within 30 to 60 minutes, reaches peak effect in about one to two hours, and is largely complete within four hours after a single oral dose in many patients. It is used for edema, not as a disease-modifying heart-failure drug. The short duration means sodium can be retained after the effect fades. Its potency, parenteral availability, and compact dose are useful, but an accidental tenfold or fortyfold conversion error can cause abrupt depletion.",
